    {
      "claim": "L4 absorption/permeability evidence for basic lipophilic amines supports adequate absorption despite the query's low neutral fraction: imipramine is essentially 100% absorbed in humans (C52, C56, C61), diphenhydramine, chlorpromazine, and promethazine show high Caco-2 absorptive permeability (C60, C62, C53, C55, C57), and nabumetone is reported well absorbed after oral administration (C59).",
      "card_ids": ["C52","C56","C61","C60","C62","C53","C55","C57","C59"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall efflux/intestinal-metabolism records do not provide affirmative low-F evidence: promethazine shows passive, non-directional transport with no significant efflux (C65, C67, C73), imipramine shows high Caco-2 permeability consistent with 100% absorption (C74), compound 16a shows high A→B permeability with an efflux ratio of 0.7 (C63, C66), and CBL0174 shows a modest B→A/A→B ratio of about 1.2 (C69, C72). Tamoxifen is identified as a P-gp substrate yet has approximately 100% human oral bioavailability (C64 with C02/C04/C05), so efflux substrate status alone is not evidence for F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C65","C67","C73","C74","C63","C66","C69","C72","C64","C02","C04","C05"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The apixaban rifampin context (C10, C15, C18, C31, C33) and its Caco-2 efflux record (C51) are not transferable low-F evidence for the query: apixaban absolute oral bioavailability was 37–39% even under strong CYP3A4/P-gp induction, and the query is a different, more lipophilic basic molecule.",
      "card_ids": ["C10","C15","C18","C31","C33","C51"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The modified-release condition affects release rate and exposure profile, but no supplied record shows that modified release reduces the extent of absorption below the 20% absolute-bioavailability threshold for this molecule class.",
      "card_ids": []
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
